# Level crossing

A **level crossing** is the step in which something that belongs to one level
of description is treated as belonging to another: a description becomes one
of the things described, or a thing described turns out to be the describer.
It is the feature that makes a loop strange. A cycle with no level crossing is
a [[feedback-loop]]; a level crossing that leads back to where it started is a
[[strange-loop]]. Hofstadter's name for a system built of such crossings is a
*tangled hierarchy*.

## Description

Every correction in the discussion comes down to this one criterion. Asked
whether a fractal is a strange loop, whether any dynamic system is one, and
whether a document that rewrites itself is one, the answer each time was the
same: it depends on whether a level is crossed, and repetition, change over
time and self-modification do not settle it.

The discussion states the criterion as the "unexpected collapse of
object-level and metalevel separation". A crossing therefore needs levels to
exist first. The schematic case has three.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[Object-level symbols] --> B[Representations of symbols]
 B --> C[Metalevel interpretation]
 C -->|refers back to| A
```

The first two arrows climb. The third is the crossing: something at the top
turns out to be about, or to act on, the bottom.

What does the crossing differs from case to case, which is why the concept is
wider than any one mechanism.

| Case | The levels | What crosses |
|---|---|---|
| Gödel | numbers; formulas; statements about provability | a number that encodes a formula, so that arithmetic speaks of its own sentences |
| Escher, *Drawing Hands* | the drawing; the hand that draws | a drawn hand that draws |
| Bach, endlessly rising canon | each key; the ascent through keys | an ascent that arrives at its starting key |
| A reflective document | card text; the rules that interpret cards | a card that describes, and then changes, those rules |

Only the first row is a formal construction. There the crossing is carried by
[[godel-numbering]], and the return by the [[diagonal-lemma]]. The other rows
cross levels with no diagonal argument at all, which is the reason a strange
loop is not the same thing as [[diagonalization]].

### Crossing as an experimental target

For the executable-document experiment the discussion names the scientific
target as "reflective level crossing, rather than recursive execution for its
own sake". A card whose cell rewrites another card has a cycle and no
crossing. The crossing would occur when a card represents the mechanism that
interprets it and the interpretation feeds back into that mechanism. Whether a
given vault achieves this has to be demonstrated case by case; running the
loop more times does not produce it.

## Relations

- It is the defining part of a [[strange-loop]] {relation: part-of}: a strange loop is a level crossing that returns.
- It presupposes [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses}, the separation that it collapses.
- It is what a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: contrasts} lacks: feedback stays on one level.
- It is not [[recursion]] {relation: distinguished-from}: recursion descends through smaller instances on the same level.
- It is not [[self-similarity]] {relation: distinguished-from}: scales of a pattern are not levels of description.
- In arithmetic it is carried by [[godel-numbering]] {relation: uses}, which lets numbers stand for formulas.
- It is wider than [[diagonalization]] {relation: generalizes}: Escher and Bach cross levels without a diagonal argument.
- The [[observer-card-experiment]] {relation: example-of} is the attempt to produce one with cards and cells.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: it is what the experiment must demonstrate.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[object-and-meta-level]].** The distinction between what a system talks
  about and talk about the system. A level crossing is the event in which that
  distinction fails to hold.
- **[[strange-loop]].** A structure that returns to its starting level after
  moving through a hierarchy. Level crossing is the single criterion that
  separates it from every other kind of loop.
- **[[godel-numbering]].** The assignment of a number to each formula and
  proof. It is the only fully formal vehicle for a level crossing that the
  discussion examines.
- **[[feedback-loop]].** Output influencing later input. It is the result of
  taking a strange loop and removing the crossing.

